====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[conscious]] [[production]] or [[arrangement]] of [[sound]]s, [[colour]]s, [[form]]s, [[movement]]s, or other [[element]]s in a manner that affects the senses and emotions, usually specifically the [[production]] of the [[beautiful]] in a [[graphic]] or [[plastic]] [[medium]].
#: {{ux|en|There is a debate as to whether graffiti is '''art''' or vandalism.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|date=May 3 1992|chapter=[[w:List of Jeeves and Wooster episodes|Comrade Bingo]]|title=w:Jeeves and Wooster|section=Series 3, Episode 6
|passage=''[[w:Bertie Wooster|B.W. Wooster]]:'' If you ask me, '''art''' is responsible for most of the trouble in the world.<br>''[[w:Jeeves|R. Jeeves]]:'' An interesting theory, sir. Would you care to [[expatiate]] upon it?<br>''[[w:Bertie Wooster|B.W. Wooster]]:'' As a matter of fact, no, Jeeves. The thought just occurred to me, as thoughts do.<br>''[[w:Jeeves|R. Jeeves]]:'' Very good, sir.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2005|month=July|author=Lynn Freed|journal=Harper's
|passage="I tell her what Donald Hall says: that the problem with workshops is that they trivialize '''art''' by minimizing the terror."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=Alexander Brouwer
|passage=Visual [[art]] is a subjective understanding or [[perception]] of the viewer as well as a deliberate/[[conscious]] arrangement or creation of elements like [[colour]]s, [[form]]s, [[movement]]s, [[sound]]s, [[object]]s or other [[element]]s that produce a [[graphic]] or plastic whole that expresses thoughts, ideas or visions of the artist.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The creative and emotional expression of mental imagery, such as visual, auditory, social, etc.
# {{lb|en|countable}} Skillful [[creative]] activity, usually with an [[aesthetic]] focus.
#: {{ux|en|She's mastered the '''art''' of programming.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[study]] and the [[product]] of these processes.
#: {{ux|en|He's at university to study '''art'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[aesthetic|Aesthetic]] [[value]].
#: {{ux|en|Her photographs are nice, but there's no '''art''' in them.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[artwork|Artwork]].
#: {{ux|en|Sotheby's regularly auctions '''art''' for millions.}}
#: {{co|en|'''art''' collection}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[field]] or [[category]] of art, such as [[painting]], [[sculpture]], [[music]], [[ballet]], or [[literature]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm a great supporter of the '''arts'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A nonscientific branch of [[learning]]; one of the [[liberal arts]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21582498-america-has-changed-way-it-measures-gdp-boundary-problems Boundary problems]
|passage=Economics is a messy discipline: too fluid to be a science, too rigorous to be an '''art'''. Perhaps it is fitting that economists’ most-used metric, gross domestic product (GDP), is a tangle too. GDP measures the total value of output in an economic territory. Its apparent simplicity explains why it is scrutinised down to tenths of a percentage point every month.}}
# {{senseid|en|studied skill}}{{lb|en|countable}} [[skill|Skill]] that is attained by [[study]], [[practice]], or [[observation]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1796|author={{w|Matthew Lewis (writer)|Matthew Lewis}}|title=w:The Monk|page=217|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=1985
|passage=A physician was immediately sent for; but on the first moment of beholding the corpse, he declared that Elvira's recovery was beyond the power of '''art'''.}}
#* '''1855''', {{w|Harriet Martineau}}'s translation, ''The Positive Philosophy of Auguste Comte''  Vol. 1, Introduction, Ch. 2, [https://archive.org/details/positivephilosop01comt/page/n68/mode/1up?view=theater page 21,] from {{w|Auguste Comte}}, ''Cours de philosophie positive'' (1830–1842)
#*: The relation of science to '''art''' may be summed up in a brief expression: From Science comes Prevision: from Prevision comes Action.
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=IV|page=58|passage=The Celebrity, by '''arts''' unknown, induced Mrs. Judge Short and two other ladies to call at Mohair on a certain afternoon when Mr. Cooke was trying a trotter on the track. The three returned wondering and charmed with Mrs. Cooke; they were sure she had had no hand in the furnishing of that atrocious house.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=03 Dec 83|volume=11|number=20|journal=Gay Community News|page=21|title=Personal advertisement|text=Let's make sandwiches out of colored paper and teach people how to listen. Listening is a social '''art''' and we had best hang on to it. A tape recording stuck in your ear won't do.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|dated}} [[contrivance|Contrivance]], scheming, manipulation.
#* {{RQ:Shelley Poetical Works|I|The Revolt of Islam|222|it was not '''art''',<br>Of wisdom and of justice when he spoke—<br>When ’mid soft looks of pity, there would dart<br>A glance as keen as is the lightning’s stroke<br>When it doth rive the knots of some ancestral oak.}}
#* {{RQ:Emily Bronte Wuthering Heights|volume=I|chapter=VI|page=112|passage=[...] and Mrs. Earnshaw undertook to keep her sister-in-law in due restraint, when she returned home employing '''art''', not force—with force she would have found it impossible.}}

